","distance":0.3098,"shared":4,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"KC","distance":0.3503,"shared":4,"play_caller":"Steve Spagnuolo"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The 49ers are 6-1 through week 7 of the 2026 season and have outscored opponents 197 to 139. Both sides of the ball have been strong, with the offense 2nd in EPA per play and the defense 9th in EPA allowed. What sets them apart is the highest pre-snap motion rate in the league.","offense":"The 49ers have one of the most efficient offenses in the league so far, ranking in the top eight in all five main efficiency measures. They have thrown more than almost anyone relative to the situation, even when the score has not called for it. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the highest pre-snap motion rate and the 2nd highest under center rate in the league. Brock Purdy has run the passing game, with George Kittle leading the team in targets.","defense":"The 49ers defense has been well above average by EPA allowed per play so far. It has been much better against the run than the pass, ranking 2nd and 15th. Keion White has led the way with 3.5 sacks. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is an eight man box rate that ranks 7th lowest in the league.","special":"The 49ers have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 67% of field goals, which is one of the worst in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into tight ends, about 8% of the money on the books. Their offensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Brock Purdy has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,748 yards. George Kittle has been the clear top target with 23% of the looks and 425 receiving yards. The backfield has been a committee led by Christian McCaffrey and Kaelon Black. On defense, Keion White leads the team with 3.5 sacks and Renardo Green has picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Kyle Shanahan is calling the offense as head coach and Raheem Morris is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2022 Lions under Ben Johnson and the 2023 Rams under Sean McVay. Their defense looks most like the 2025 Panthers under Ejiro Evero and the 2025 Commanders.","trends":"The offense went from 6th in EPA per play in 2025 to 2nd so far this season, a modest improvement. The defense moved from 24th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 9th so far this season, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was blitz rate, which went from 22% to 32%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}